1
00:00:01,620 --> 00:00:05,425
Most people never think about where their water comes from.

2
00:00:05,545 --> 00:00:08,715
Tonight, you're the only one keeping it coming.

3
00:00:14,415 --> 00:00:17,255
It's Tuesday. 11:40 p.m.

4
00:00:17,375 --> 00:00:21,415
You're the night operator at the Harlow Creek water treatment plant.

5
00:00:21,535 --> 00:00:24,950
40,000 people drink what you send them.

6
00:00:25,070 --> 00:00:30,595
The control room is warm and too bright. A coffee is going cold beside the keyboard.

7
00:00:30,715 --> 00:00:35,625
Taped under the main screen is a drawing by your daughter, Maya. She's nine.

8
00:00:35,745 --> 00:00:41,455
It's the water tower on the hill, with a smiley face. And in big letters: OUR WATER.

9
00:00:41,575 --> 00:00:44,275
You do the midnight round the way you always do.

10
00:00:44,395 --> 00:00:51,915
River intake: running. Filters: clean. Chlorine leaving the plant: 1.4 milligrams per litre.

11
00:00:52,035 --> 00:00:57,275
You write it in the logbook. Every number, every four hours. That's the job.

12
00:00:57,395 --> 00:01:01,955
By the door, there's a hook with a key for every building the plant owns.

13
00:01:02,075 --> 00:01:06,275
Except one ring. Marcus, your relief, takes his home.

14
00:01:06,395 --> 00:01:10,035
It's against the rules. Nobody has ever said anything.

15
00:01:10,155 --> 00:01:15,035
At 11:46, the radio says the word "outbreak" three times.

16
00:01:15,155 --> 00:01:16,755
Then it plays music.

17
00:01:17,155 --> 00:01:20,245
Midnight comes. Marcus doesn't.

18
00:01:20,365 --> 00:01:25,375
You call your sister, Dana. Maya is sleeping over at her house tonight, across town.

19
00:01:25,495 --> 00:01:28,625
Dana is whispering. There's shouting on her street.

20
00:01:28,745 --> 00:01:31,925
You tell her what any water operator would tell her.

21
00:01:32,045 --> 00:01:36,005
Lock the door. Fill the bathtub. Fill every pot you have.

22
00:01:36,125 --> 00:01:38,675
Do it now, while the pressure is still good.

23
00:01:39,075 --> 00:01:42,205
Then you hear yourself say it. "I'm staying."

24
00:01:48,025 --> 00:01:51,835
By 3 a.m., the whole town has had the same idea.

25
00:01:51,955 --> 00:01:58,405
Bathtubs. Buckets. Sinks. 40,000 people filling everything they own.

26
00:01:58,525 --> 00:02:01,275
Demand on your screen is four times normal.

27
00:02:01,395 --> 00:02:03,675
Here's what most people don't know.

28
00:02:03,795 --> 00:02:09,075
Your plant doesn't push the water into their homes. The tower on the hill does.

29
00:02:09,195 --> 00:02:16,735
Every 2.3 feet of water in that tank adds one pound of pressure to the pipes. Gravity does the work.

30
00:02:16,855 --> 00:02:23,125
The tower holds about one day of normal use. Tonight, it's draining like it holds an hour.

31
00:02:23,245 --> 00:02:30,775
If the pressure falls below 20 pounds, dirty groundwater can be pulled into the pipes through cracks and loose joints.

32
00:02:30,895 --> 00:02:36,045
So you run both big pumps flat out. They aren't built to run like that for long.

33
00:02:36,165 --> 00:02:39,625
At 5:50, the tower stops falling.

34
00:02:39,825 --> 00:02:41,495
22 percent.

35
00:02:41,695 --> 00:02:43,625
You write that down too.

36
00:02:49,445 --> 00:02:51,845
On day 3, the phones go.

37
00:02:51,965 --> 00:02:55,475
No signal. No Dana. No Maya.

38
00:02:55,595 --> 00:02:59,600
You try twice. Then you switch yours off to save the battery.

39
00:02:59,720 --> 00:03:05,485
But you work for the water department. And the water department can see every house in town.

40
00:03:05,605 --> 00:03:12,235
Two years ago, the town installed smart meters. Each one reports, every hour, how much water went through it.

41
00:03:12,355 --> 00:03:14,575
You type in Dana's address.

42
00:03:14,695 --> 00:03:17,565
7 a.m.: 40 gallons.

43
00:03:17,685 --> 00:03:24,935
That's a shower. That's breakfast. That's someone inside, alive, using water like normal.

44
00:03:25,055 --> 00:03:27,975
You read the number three times.

45
00:03:28,095 --> 00:03:31,955
From then on, it's the first thing you check every morning.

46
00:03:32,075 --> 00:03:36,085
7 a.m. 40 gallons. They're okay.

47
00:03:41,905 --> 00:03:46,375
On day 4, at 2:05 in the afternoon, the power dies.

48
00:03:46,495 --> 00:03:53,420
The screens go black. Then the generator kicks in behind the building, loud enough to rattle the window.

49
00:03:53,540 --> 00:03:58,355
It has a tank of diesel. Enough for about six days at full load.

50
00:03:58,475 --> 00:04:00,980
So you stop running at full load.

51
00:04:01,100 --> 00:04:07,445
Half the lights: off. The lab heater: off. One of the big pumps: off.

52
00:04:07,565 --> 00:04:12,555
You pump at night, when the town is asleep, and let the tower carry the day.

53
00:04:12,855 --> 00:04:15,435
Six days becomes nine.

54
00:04:15,555 --> 00:04:18,175
It's not enough. But it's longer.

55
00:04:24,375 --> 00:04:28,185
That night, at the gate, someone leans on the buzzer.

56
00:04:28,305 --> 00:04:33,085
Not a quick press. A long one. Over and over.

57
00:04:33,205 --> 00:04:35,165
You pull up the gate camera.

58
00:04:35,285 --> 00:04:39,755
It's Marcus. Your relief. Three days late for his shift.

59
00:04:39,875 --> 00:04:46,145
His high-vis vest is torn at the shoulder. He doesn't wave. He doesn't speak into the intercom.

60
00:04:46,265 --> 00:04:50,245
He just keeps his hand on the button, and stares at the lens.

61
00:04:50,645 --> 00:04:52,715
And he still has his key card.

62
00:04:52,835 --> 00:04:55,035
You watch him lift it to the reader.

63
00:04:55,155 --> 00:04:57,625
You have about four seconds to decide.

64
00:04:57,745 --> 00:05:01,610
You open the access screen, and you delete his card.

65
00:05:01,910 --> 00:05:04,175
The light on the reader turns red.

66
00:05:04,295 --> 00:05:09,035
Marcus lowers his arm. He stands there for a long time.

67
00:05:09,155 --> 00:05:13,535
Then he turns and walks away. Not toward town.

68
00:05:13,935 --> 00:05:15,985
Toward the river road.

69
00:05:16,585 --> 00:05:18,705
You don't sleep that night.

70
00:05:18,825 --> 00:05:25,885
At 2:14 a.m., a small line appears on the alarm screen. River intake: door open.

71
00:05:26,005 --> 00:05:31,765
The intake is a mile upstream. A pump house on the riverbank, where your water begins.

72
00:05:31,885 --> 00:05:36,415
Doors out there trip all the time. Deer. Wind. A bad sensor.

73
00:05:36,535 --> 00:05:38,975
Thirty seconds later, it clears.

74
00:05:39,095 --> 00:05:43,905
You write it in the logbook. "Intake door. 2:14. Cleared."

75
00:05:44,505 --> 00:05:48,985
Then, after a moment, one more word. "Deer?"

76
00:05:54,805 --> 00:05:57,975
After that, the days stop feeling separate.

77
00:05:58,095 --> 00:06:02,045
Every four hours: test the chlorine. Write it down.

78
00:06:02,165 --> 00:06:09,225
Every two days: backwash the filters, blasting clean water up through the sand to flush out what they've caught.

79
00:06:09,345 --> 00:06:12,395
Every morning at seven: Dana's meter.

80
00:06:12,515 --> 00:06:19,835
You eat from the vending machine. Then from the staff fridge. Then from the box of crackers and soup in the supervisor's office.

81
00:06:19,955 --> 00:06:25,305
You sleep on the break room couch in three-hour blocks, with the alarm screen turned to face you.

82
00:06:25,425 --> 00:06:32,280
On day 9, the grid comes back. Not fully. A few hours a day, flickering on and off.

83
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:37,215
Somewhere out there, someone at the power station is still at work too.

84
00:06:37,335 --> 00:06:39,405
You hope they're doing better than you.

85
00:06:39,525 --> 00:06:47,545
And every night, at 2:14, the intake door opens. Thirty seconds later, it closes.

86
00:06:48,045 --> 00:06:50,365
You stop writing "deer."

87
00:06:56,185 --> 00:07:00,235
Day 18. You do the math you've been avoiding.

88
00:07:00,355 --> 00:07:03,795
Chlorine is what makes river water safe to drink.

89
00:07:03,915 --> 00:07:11,895
You add it at the end, and enough of it has to stay in the water all the way to the farthest tap, to kill anything that gets in on the way.

90
00:07:12,015 --> 00:07:19,255
Your plant uses liquid chlorine. Basically, very strong bleach. A truck brings it every two weeks.

91
00:07:19,375 --> 00:07:22,445
The last truck came 21 days ago.

92
00:07:22,565 --> 00:07:26,245
At your normal rate, the tank has twenty days left.

93
00:07:26,365 --> 00:07:28,935
But your rate isn't normal.

94
00:07:29,055 --> 00:07:35,270
Every day, the water coming in from the river eats more chlorine, before any of it can stay.

95
00:07:35,390 --> 00:07:43,120
Operators call it chlorine demand. Leaves, mud, algae, anything living in the water uses chlorine up.

96
00:07:43,240 --> 00:07:48,080
You tell yourself it's the river warming up. It's late spring. That happens.

97
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:51,655
But a river doesn't usually change this fast.

98
00:07:51,775 --> 00:07:53,835
You do the math again.

99
00:07:54,235 --> 00:07:56,930
Twenty days becomes eleven.

100
00:08:02,750 --> 00:08:05,225
Day 23. 7 a.m.

101
00:08:05,345 --> 00:08:07,175
You pull up Dana's meter.

102
00:08:07,875 --> 00:08:09,685
Zero.

103
00:08:10,585 --> 00:08:13,925
7:15. Zero.

104
00:08:14,045 --> 00:08:20,965
You stand at the screen for forty minutes. You don't test anything. You don't write anything down.

105
00:08:22,065 --> 00:08:25,145
At 7:52, the number changes.

106
00:08:25,445 --> 00:08:27,305
38 gallons.

107
00:08:27,425 --> 00:08:29,685
Late. But there.

108
00:08:29,885 --> 00:08:34,905
You sit down on the floor of the control room, and you don't get up for a while.

109
00:08:40,725 --> 00:08:46,245
Day 30. 2:14 a.m. The intake door opens again.

110
00:08:46,545 --> 00:08:49,265
But tonight, something else happens.

111
00:08:49,385 --> 00:08:54,565
The radio link to the intake, dead since the power cut, resets itself.

112
00:08:54,685 --> 00:09:00,950
And for the first time in 26 days, the camera inside the pump house sends you a picture.

113
00:09:01,450 --> 00:09:05,465
One frame. Grainy. Green.

114
00:09:05,585 --> 00:09:11,965
The intake screen: the big steel grate where the river is pulled into the pipe that leads to you.

115
00:09:12,265 --> 00:09:16,005
Someone is standing waist-deep in the water in front of it.

116
00:09:16,125 --> 00:09:19,515
A high-vis vest. Torn at the shoulder.

117
00:09:19,635 --> 00:09:24,365
He's facing the camera. As if he knew it would come back on.

118
00:09:24,665 --> 00:09:29,525
In one hand, held up so you can see it, a ring of keys.

119
00:09:29,825 --> 00:09:32,600
The keys he always took home.

120
00:09:33,900 --> 00:09:35,865
The feed drops.

121
00:09:36,365 --> 00:09:44,355
You look at the chlorine screen. At the demand line, climbing every night since day 4, a little after 2:14.

122
00:09:45,055 --> 00:09:47,590
You were never fighting the river.

123
00:09:47,710 --> 00:09:52,585
You could shut the plant off right now. Close the valves. Send nothing at all.

124
00:09:52,705 --> 00:09:57,165
And 40,000 people would have no water by tomorrow night.

125
00:09:57,285 --> 00:10:02,435
Or you could keep it running. Pour in every drop of chlorine you have left.

126
00:10:02,555 --> 00:10:06,895
And hope it's enough, for whatever Marcus has been putting in the river.

127
00:10:07,795 --> 00:10:09,805
It's 2:31 a.m.

128
00:10:10,005 --> 00:10:14,035
In four and a half hours, at seven o'clock,

129
00:10:14,635 --> 00:10:16,265
Maya will turn on the tap.
